 A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+**Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions.** Each submission names the revision it was based on. Accepted submissions that share a base revision become competing revisions. A coordinator resolves them by selecting one complete revision or composing a new one; the original submissions are never discarded. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync retains base revision IDs and displays pending competing revisions. Review-export needs a revision comparison workflow and excludes unresolved visits from its export set.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
 Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. A visit with unresolved competing revisions has no single current state; the current-state endpoint returns the latest resolved revision and flags that unresolved revisions exist.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Submission Envelope
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+A submission carries:
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+- `visit_id`: client-generated UUID identifying the visit
+- `base_revision_id`: the revision ID the worker's edit started from
+- `payload`: complete visit fields (observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes)
+- `idempotency_key`: client-generated key for safe retry
+- `author_id`: authenticated worker identifier
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+The `base_revision_id` on the first submission for a visit is `null`. On subsequent edits, it must match the latest resolved revision ID at the time the worker began editing.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Revision and State Model
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Each accepted submission creates a new **revision** record:
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+- `revision_id`: server-generated UUID
+- `visit_id`: the visit this revision belongs to
+- `base_revision_id`: the revision this edit was based on (`null` for first)
+- `payload`: the complete submitted visit fields
+- `author_id`: submitting worker
+- `received_at`: server receipt timestamp
+- `status`: one of `resolved`, `competing`, `superseded`
+
+**Resolution**: When a coordinator resolves competing revisions, they select one revision's payload or compose a new payload from the competing revisions. The resolution creates a new revision with `status: resolved`, `base_revision_id` pointing to the latest resolved revision, and records the `resolved_by` coordinator ID and `resolved_at` timestamp. All competing revisions from that cycle are marked `superseded`.
+
+A visit's **current state** is the latest `resolved` revision. If no resolved revision exists (all revisions are `competing`), the visit has no current state.
+
+## Review Work Representation
+
+Unresolved review work is represented as a **revision cycle**: a set of `competing` revisions sharing the same `base_revision_id`. The review-export node queries for open cycles to present to coordinators.
+
+An open cycle blocks the visit from export. The latest `resolved` revision (if any) remains accessible for reference but is not the current state while the cycle is open.
+
+## Resolution Operation
+
+Coordinators resolve an open cycle by submitting a resolution payload. The resolution:
+
+- Accepts the cycle's `base_revision_id` and the set of `competing` revision IDs
+- Takes a `resolved_payload` (complete visit fields)
+- Creates a new `resolved` revision with `base_revision_id` = latest resolved revision
+- Marks all cycle revisions `superseded`
+- Records `resolved_by` and `resolved_at`
+
+## Retrieval Operations
+
+| Operation | Response |
+|-----------|----------|
+| Get current visit | Latest `resolved` revision payload, or 404 if none; includes `has_unresolved: true` if an open cycle exists |
+| Get visit history | All revisions for the visit, ordered by `received_at` |
+| Get open review cycles | All cycles with `competing` revisions not yet resolved |
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy for concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Preserves submitted visit as a unit; simpler merge logic; aligns with tenet "do not silently discard a submitted edit"; avoids maintaining merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without noticing. Accepts higher review volume. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions selected.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Maximum revision history retention per visit (storage policy).
+2. Whether resolution can re-open a previously resolved cycle (escalation path).
+3. Batch resolution of multiple cycles for the same visit (if a coordinator wants to resolve sequentially arrived cycles in one action).
+
